<p>El Salvador has opened a collective trial against 486 alleged MS-13 members, including senior figures from the gang’s national leadership, in one of the country’s biggest prosecutions under President Nayib Bukele’s emergency security campaign. Prosecutors say the case covers more than 47,000 crimes committed between 2012 and 2022. It also includes allegations that gang leaders ordered the killings of 86 people during the March 2022 weekend that led the government to impose a state of emergency, later extended 49 times. The trial shows how El Salvador is trying to sustain a sweeping crackdown that authorities describe as a response to years of gang violence, while critics say the collective proceedings weaken due process and limit access to legal counsel. The case also comes as the Salvadoran attorney general’s office says it will begin using a “Sovereign AI Platform” to receive, structure and evaluate citizen complaints, extending the government’s technology push into the justice system alongside its security policies.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/oKqAfxDr8c1A_4IMBaMffyjx-qDxzJ35jIhlaIIKjII3ILtCgC7Rchm0yua_o72tbCMBIdxASbuZQa-osE7093n8vx-hEKJXPCPE7seKElops5pTNFNuFoejZDEPPVx2hwb59kQXtntcxsB9j5cvTozohxvDbsB57lcgjNgd-w' alt='Alleged MS-13 members appear in court during the mass trial in El Salvador.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Court format: The defendants are being tried together in a collective proceeding instead of in hundreds of separate cases, a practice human rights groups say makes individual defense harder.</li><li>Crime span: Prosecutors say the indictment ties the accused to offenses committed over a decade, from 2012 through 2022, showing the breadth of the case beyond a single attack.</li><li>Leadership focus: Those on trial include members of the Ranfla Nacional, the gang’s top leadership structure, which authorities accuse of directing major acts of violence.</li><li>Tech rollout: At the SovAI 2026 conference in San Salvador, the attorney general’s office announced an AI-assisted criminal complaint system meant to help receive and assess reports from citizens.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Salvadoran prosecutors: Prosecutors are treating the case as a broad effort to hold MS-13 leaders responsible for more than 47,000 crimes over ten years, including ordering the March 2022 killings that preceded emergency rule. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/apr/22/el-salvador-mass-trial-alleged-members-ms-13-gang'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Human rights groups: Human rights organizations warn that collective prosecutions violate due process protections and can keep defendants from meeting lawyers or mounting individualized defenses. (<a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/500-alleged-ms-13-members-el-salvador-face-132251825'>ABC News</a>)</li><li>Bukele government: The government is pairing its security policy with new digital tools, with the attorney general’s office presenting AI systems for complaint handling as part of a broader modernization effort. (<a href='https://english.elpais.com/international/2026-04-22/el-salvador-tries-nearly-500-mara-salvatrucha-gang-leaders-amid-international-criticism.html'>El País</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://english.elpais.com/international/2026-04-22/el-salvador-tries-nearly-500-mara-salvatrucha-gang-leaders-amid-international-criticism.html'>El Salvador tries nearly 500 Mara Salvatrucha gang leaders amid international criticism</a> - elpais.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/apr/22/el-salvador-mass-trial-alleged-members-ms-13-gang'>El Salvador holds mass trial for 486 alleged members of notorious MS-13 gang</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/500-alleged-ms-13-members-el-salvador-face-132251825'>Nearly 500 alleged MS-13 members are on a mass trial in El Salvador</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://japantoday.com/category/world/el-salvador-court-tries-over-400-alleged-gang-leaders-for-47-000-crimes'>El Salvador court tries over 400 alleged gang leaders for 47,000 crimes</a> - japantoday.com</li></ul>